## Dune bashing, in one paragraph

**Dune bashing** is what happens when a driver takes a four-wheel-drive vehicle off the road and into a dune field at speed. The vehicle goes up the gentle windward side of a dune, runs along the crest, and comes down the steep, soft side at an angle that looks wrong from inside the cabin. Repeat for half an hour across a sea of sand. Guests sit in the back, belted in, and the driver does all the work. Nobody "bashes" anything; the name is older than the tourism industry and comes from Gulf drivers doing it for fun on weekends.

## How it works on our safaris

Our Land Cruisers leave from our base at Al Awir on the Dubai-Hatta road and head into the **Lahbab red dunes**, about 45 minutes from Downtown. Before the vehicle goes onto the sand, the driver lets air out of the tyres. Softer tyres spread the weight over more sand, which is what stops a two-tonne vehicle digging itself in on the first climb. They are pumped back up before the vehicle returns to the road.

Then the driver works the dunes. Roughly twenty to forty minutes, depending on the package, the state of the sand that day and how the people in the back are taking it. Our drivers run the same dune field every day, so they know which faces are firm, which ones shift after wind, and where the sunset stop will be when the light starts going.

After that the drive calms down. You stop on a ridge for photographs, then carry on to the camp for the camel ride, sandboarding, dinner and the shows. If you want the full order of the day, the [evening desert safari](/desert-safari-dubai/evening-desert-safari/) page walks through it.

{% figure image="camel-ride-desert-safari-dubai" caption="After the dunes, the drive calms down and the camp takes over: camel ride first, then dinner and the shows." /%}

## What it actually feels like

Like a rollercoaster with no track. The climbs push you into the seat. The drops are the part people scream at, because for a second the bonnet points at the sky and then at the ground. Sideways traverses along a dune face tilt the cabin more than you expect, and sand sprays over the windows.

The honest part: it is bumpy, it is loud, and a few people in every week's bookings feel sick by the end of it. Eating a heavy lunch right before the pickup does not help. Sitting in the front, when there is room, helps a lot, because you can see what is coming.

## Morning or evening dune bashing

| | Morning | Evening |
|---|---|---|
| Sand | Cooler and firmer | Softer after a day of sun |
| Heat | Mild, even in warmer months | Hot at pickup, easing toward sunset |
| What follows | Camel ride, sandboarding, back before lunch | Sunset stop, camp, dinner and shows |
| Who books it | Early risers, photographers, people avoiding heat | Most guests, families, first-timers |

Our drivers generally prefer the morning: firmer sand means the vehicle behaves more predictably. The evening is still what most people book, because it flows straight into the camp. The [morning desert safari](/desert-safari-dubai/morning-desert-safari/) is the quieter alternative.

## Who should skip it

We do not recommend dune bashing for:

- Anyone who is pregnant
- Anyone with back, neck or heart problems, or recent surgery
- Anyone who gets badly motion sick on winding roads
- Very young children, if you are not sure how they will take it

Skipping it does not mean skipping the safari. Ask for the calm route when you book, the driver takes a gentle line to the camp, and the price stays the same. We have written a full guide on [booking a desert safari without dune bashing](/blogs/dubai-desert-safari-without-dune-bashing/), including the exact line to add to your message.

{% figure image="sandboarding-desert-safari-dubai" caption="Skipping the dune bashing still leaves sandboarding, the camel ride and the camp." /%}

On a private safari the vehicle is yours, so the driver can do more dune bashing, less of it, or a mix within one group. That flexibility is the main reason families pick the [private desert safari](/desert-safari-dubai/private-desert-safari/).

## Dune bashing vs driving a buggy yourself

People mix these up. In dune bashing you are a passenger in a 4x4 and a professional is driving. On a [dune buggy](/dune-buggy-dubai/) you drive the machine yourself, with a helmet, a briefing and a guide leading the group across the same red dunes. Minimum age to drive a buggy is 14, and pricing is per vehicle, from AED 300.

{% figure image="dune-buggy-climbing-red-dune-convoy-dubai" caption="A self-drive buggy convoy on the same red dunes. Here you are the one working the throttle." /%}

They feel completely different. In the buggy your attention is on the throttle, the sand and the rider in front. In the Land Cruiser you have nothing to do except react, which is why the drops feel steeper. Plenty of groups book both: the drivers take a buggy session, then everyone meets in the 4x4 for the dune bashing and the camp.

## What it costs

Dune bashing is not sold on its own. It comes inside every standard safari package. The shared evening safari is **AED 99 per person**; private safaris are **per vehicle**, from AED 650, which often works out cheaper for a group of five or more. Hotel pickup and drop-off inside Dubai is free on every safari. All twelve packages are on the [desert safari price page](/desert-safari-dubai/price/).
